# Words a garden may also use that are nonetheless PUBLIC, so `bin/public.py` does not refuse them.
# One per line, with the reason. Everything the published classifications name (seed/knowledge/*.tsv) is
# already public and needs no entry here: this file is for the rest, and each line is a decision.
# `<word> <path> ...` makes the word public IN THOSE FILES only (paths as git spells them): a consent given for
# one page is not a consent for every file, commit message and pull request.
daftar      # the project itself
daftard     # the language's own program (daftar-hub's daemon): a garden that names a being by it took the name from the
            # program — the operator's word, 2026-10-12, as the guard asks ("PUBLIC-ALLOW `daftard`")
ups         # an uninterruptible power supply: the device's ordinary word, which the law's quantities speak of (what a
            # UPS is rated in); a garden that names its UPS so took the word from the language — the operator's word,
            # 2026-10-12
0mit        # the account this repository is published under
walk-run    # the language's own walk of a run (bin/run.py `setup` writes it into a garden's mappings, core 2.11): a
            # garden's mapping of that name took it from the tool — the standard walk by the operator's word, 2026-10-18
            # ("yes we move them", runs into daftar)
walk-transfer  # the language's own walk of a transfer (assets/storage transfer.py `setup` writes it into a garden's
               # mappings, core 2.19): a garden's mapping of that name took it from the tool, as `walk-run` — the
               # operator's word, 2026-10-20 ("Allow `walk-transfer`"), as the guard asked
runs        # an ordinary word, and the language's own tool's (programme item 2 makes runs daftar's `bin/run.py`, its
            # registry the store `runs`): a garden that names a being after the tool took the word from it — the
            # operator's word, 2026-10-18 ("Allow `runs`"), as the guard asked
daftar-sessions  # this tool's OWN directory name (bin/session.py); a garden that records a root of
                 # that name took the name FROM the tool, so refusing it here would be the tail wagging
                 # the dog — and renaming it, as was tried first, orphans every existing worktree.
omid README.md LICENSE NOTICE REUSE.toml CHARTER.md seed/LICENSE.md  # the steward, named where the terms need a name: README by his word, 2026-09-22 ("the persian part is by me"); the licence files by his word, 2026-09-25 ("I do"), with his registered name and his address
omid@0mit.com CHARTER.md  # the steward's address, where a claim about what he publishes reaches him — by his word, 2026-09-25
0mit.com      CHARTER.md  # the domain whose published contacts also reach him, while he holds it — by his word, 2026-09-25
held             # the law's own word: a layer (std-vocab `layers`), the key of a sealed entry, and bin/held.py's
                 # name. A garden that names its store root after the layer took the name FROM the law, as
                 # `daftar-sessions` above was taken from a tool.
agents-work      # the law's own name: the bean of the gardener's standing instructions to agents, which
                 # seed/agents-work.md.template makes and core/guards.py and core/judge.py read by that name; a
                 # garden that holds the bean took the name from the law.
rights@ilo.org seed/knowledge/SOURCES.md  # the International Labour Organization's published address for the
                 # rights in its works, named where ISCO-08's source says whom it asked.
smb              # a row of the law's own `net_protocols` registry: a garden that names a bean after the protocol
                 # took the name from the law.
git@github.com   # GitHub's SSH user and host, the same for every repository there: a remote begins with it and
                 # names no one. The address pattern reads it as an email address.
192.168.0.0/24   # RFC 1918's private range, the default network of countless routers: it identifies no estate,
                 # and the law's reasons take it as their example of one network inside another.
192.168.0.0      # the same range's network address.
10.0.0.0/8       # RFC 1918's three private blocks, as the RFC itself names them: a reason or a test that says which
172.16.0.0/12    # addresses are private names a standard, not an estate (T1 of the second build run: the leak guard
192.168.0.0/16   # refused the third, and a stage wrote it as a number instead).
fc00::/7         # RFC 4193's unique local addresses, the same for IPv6.
std-vocab@2.0    # the law's own versions, as this repository's history names them: a garden that records the
                 # version it ran names the law, not itself. The address pattern reads `name@version` as an
                 # email address.
std-vocab@2.1    # the same.
std-vocab@5.1    # the same.
13.0.0.0 test/calendars.py  # the Maya long count's 13.0.0.0.0 (2012-12-21): a date, not an address
marcusolsson-dynamictext-panel@6.3.0 test/view.py  # a published Grafana plugin at a published version, as the view's test fixture names it
